# BACKEND · what must not enter the build context
#
# Short and hand-kept on purpose: everything in fl_backend/ not listed here reaches the image,
# because the runner copies the context wholesale, so this list answers "what does the image
# contain?". Add a line when something here is a secret, a machine-local artifact, or a file
# nothing at run time reads. `**/` where a name can appear at any depth: a bare `__pycache__/`
# matches the context root alone and would leave app/**/__pycache__ in the image, while `**/`
# also matches zero directories, so the root copy needs no second line.

# Matched by shape, not inventory: a rule holds whether or not anything matches it now, because
# a credential inside an image is a failure nobody sees until it ships. Kept identically in every
# `.dockerignore` here, so no build context is weaker.
**/.env
**/.env.*
**/*.pem
**/*.key
**/*.crt
**/.npmrc

# The developer's virtualenv, built against another platform's interpreter. The image's own comes
# from the builder stage.
.venv/

**/__pycache__/
**/*.py[cod]
**/.pytest_cache/
**/.ruff_cache/
**/*.log

# `uv sync --no-dev` installs no pytest, so the suite could only ever be inert bytes in the image.
tests/

# The published document the frontend's contract test compares against (ADR-0033). The running app
# serves its OpenAPI from the live routes, so this copy is a repository artifact, not runtime input.
openapi.json

# buildkit receives both by other means; copying them in only invalidates layers.
Dockerfile
.dockerignore
